seems like going early would sort  of reduce that and then I would say the  second big thing is my fiancee lives in  New York City and so that was a big  decision because I had the opportunity  to move with Google to New York City and  be with him or obviously come to  Stanford and be  be a part and so I think that that was  definitely a huge huge consideration but  the GSB has a fantastic significant  significant other network and has really  brought him in he's actually really good  friends with Mike now through the GSB so  he's been extremely welcomed into this  community and he actually has had lots  of interviews with GSB alums that I've  set up for him through the alumni  network as well  I actually quick story emailed about ten  people in real estate in New York City  on his behalf and all ten replied so  just another story about the power of  the alumni network and yes it's an  opportunity cost but I think we'll be  stronger for having had the exposure  we've had while here that's great so  speaking of the exposure you've had  while you're here I was wondering if  each of you could talk a little bit  about the academic experience Mike if  you want to start with maybe a favorite  class or an experience that stands out  to you specifically around the academics  sure sure well as I mentioned before one  of the reasons why I came to business  school was to really learn how to be  this type of inspirational motivational  leader that my first manager was coming  out of college and I think the GSB does  an incredible job of giving you sort of  real-life experience quote unquote real  life but expert experiential learning  opportunities to do that and one class  that I will remember as the most  impactful class that I had was  interpersonal dynamics also known as  touchy feely which is a class where you  really explore how you react to other  people but also how you come off to  other people and you know I would say  before the GSB I was always very afraid  to truly open up to people about how I  felt how they made me feel what my  impact was on them what their impact was  on me but this is a sort of class that  creates a safe space for people to  really ask those tough questions of each  other and give each other honest  feedback about those impacts and so I  would say that the experiential learning  especially through classes like  touchy-feely has been the just the most  amazing part of the academic curriculum  here Patrick can you expand a little bit  on that what proportion would you say of  classes kind of break down different  teaching styles how much time would you  spend on cases versus lecturers versus  experiential learning and a little bit  more on the tactical side is it hard to  get electives or do you get to take the  classes that you want sure yeah so I  would say first year was more lecture  and case based for me and I feel free to  jump in if you disagree but first year  was it was probably about half and half  so for some of my classes like strategy  we we were in a case based format for  others like accounting it was a little  bit more lecture based although there  were some cases that were that we're  done and so the professor's basically  decided what would be the best way for  folks to learn the material that were  teaching and the most effective way so  that was mostly about half and half for  the elective second-year a lot more of  them have been experiential for me  because again I've been focusing on  coaching and interpersonal dynamics and  I'm part of a program called called  Arbuckle fellows and so that shift  second-year has been mostly to  experiential and then still still some  lecture and case base mixed in but  probably about 60% experiential and the  rest  Kayson and lecture based ok great so you  mentioned Arbuckle leadership fellows  maybe you can talk a little bit about  what that is and then both of you can  also add on other extracurriculars that  you're involved with in other  commitments that you have outside of  coursework in school and also how much  time you actually do spend on homework  and classes relative to that sure yeah  so Arbuckle fellows is a coaching  program that was started I want to say  about 10 years now but I'm not exactly  sure if that's the right date and  basically it allows the second year  class to coach and lead the first years  through what we call leadership labs  which is a first year required class in  your first quarter that allows you to  think about what kind of leader you want  to be if you're the kind of leader you  aren't how do you get there learning  from other leadership styles that other  students have that you can sort of learn  from and so you as an our buckle fellow  lead six students in that first quarter  class and then you switch into  one-on-one coaching for the winter term  and so you actually get assigned three  cochise that you work with each week for  an hour working through all sorts of  topics so it ranges from leadership  topics to how do i balance my life at  the GSB academics and and social and  career to just personal stuff that's  going on so that's been my most  fulfilling activity at the GSB a huge  chunk of time but a really great way to  practice those leadership and coaching  skills that I came here to to work on  awesome Mike how do you spend your time  outside of class or there's particular  clubs that you're really involved with  yeah yeah I can so I can talk to that so  I spent a lot of my time actually  helping TA classes at the GSB especially  in the second year and it's actually one  of the things that I've personally found  and a most rewarding about the Stanford  experience which is that so much of the  learning that happens here happens  because we have just incredible  classmates who come from all types of  different backgrounds and so because I  have a finance investing background I  have spent a lot of my time this year  helping to of the finance professors  here teach classes TA classes develop  case material and that that's sort of  been my big commitment outside of  regular coursework and it's something  that I do because I think you know one  it's it's good practice in terms of  learning how to be a leader not to  communicate how to teach people but at  the same time it's it's allowed me to  give back to the community that I think  has already given me a lot over the past  two years  in terms of the the split between times  spent on different activities I would  say it varies pretty dramatically so  it's very hard to say you know what the  average is because it's very  unrepresentative of the variance is very  large and so I would say that the great  thing about the GSP is that if you come  there is no structure to which you have  to adhere if you want to spend a lot of  time on academics and I know I feel like  I've probably indexed on that relatively  more heavily than some of our classmates  then you can do that if you want to  spend more of your time on  extracurriculars more of your time on  even social activities that's that's  completely accepted and and even  encouraged at Stanford so I think it's  really hard to say what a typical GSB  student looks like because there is no  typical GSB student like everyone has  different priorities and all those  priorities are welcomed by the community  so we've talked about learning in the  classroom could each of you talk about  one of the global experiences or maybe  multiple that you've been involved with  and how that's shaped your GSB  experience sure yeah so I participated  in a GST so a global study trip to  Thailand last winter and so the global  study trips are led by second years so  we were I was a first year at the time  so we were taken to Thailand by five  second years who totally crafted the  experience and chose to look at the  country through food so we studied the  sort of the food supply chain in  Thailand we looked at we visited a local  rice paddy but we also looked at went  and visited some of the largest  conglomerates that are actually  packaging and and just distributing all  over the world and so we just had a  chance to explore a new culture meet  with all sorts of people again from  someone running their own small shop to  someone running a huge conglomerate and  we're able to do it together so it was  definitely for me I thought  it allowed me to see the country in a  whole new way because we were very on  the ground and and sort of seeing it  from all these different angles but also  it really allowed me to forge incredible  friendships through just by being in a  country in a totally new place with  folks that I didn't know super well  before the trip and so I think I left  with a whole new appreciation for the  country of Thailand but also for a lot  of my classmates who I may not have  otherwise gotten to know yeah I had a  similar experience  the trip that I remember most vividly is  actually one that was organized by our  classmates to India and we spent about  10 days there met with everyone ranging  from political leaders to small business  owners to executives at larger  corporations and learn a lot about India  developed a new sense of appreciation  for the amount of growth and potential  in the country I think that stuff is you  know you can get that stuff just like  going to different countries and  traveling on your own what's what's  really unique about the trips that you  take at the GSB to these countries is  that you're going with classmates I  think something like 40% of our class is  international students and so you know  in all these countries the people who  are leading you the people who are  showing you the country are people from  those places who either grew up there  went to school there or worked there and  have incredible networks and just an  incredible depth of understanding of  those places and I think that's what's  really unique about these trips you know  it's not the fact that you can go all  over the world it's the fact you can go  with someone who knows those places  intimately and that's been the most I  think meaningful part of the  international experience for me at the  GSB  yeah our trip leader to Thailand  actually was from Thailand and so we had  big family dinners with her family who  live over there and got to see meet  leaders who we would we would not  otherwise have had access to so I think  that's a a really good point about  seeing the country through someone's  eyes who's actually experienced it day  to day yeah  great so some of the exploration happens  through global experiences I think a lot  of self-exploration also happens at the  GSB and when you think about exploring  your career opportunities have you used  any specific resources at Stanford to  help you navigate that kind of  exploration I know you mentioned  possibly doing a career change so how  has that been influenced by the GSB and  opportunities through the MBA program  yeah I would say for me it was mostly  those informational interviews that has  shaped that again the access to the  online alumni network also we have a  fantastic Career Center so I basically  took those informational interviews had  like two buckets that I wanted to focus  on for my summer internship and and for  longer term and the Career Center pulled  up a whole nother list of alums in those  spaces helped me think through a  timeline to actually think about trying  different things during the year and I'm  actually doing an internship so as I  mentioned I'm joint degree student in  Edden and in business and I'm actually  doing an internship with Stanford  undergrads with what through the res  life Department to explore that higher  opportunity so that that's possible you  get course credits through through the  GSB so I'm actually getting credit for  that internship and so you don't just  have the the summer experience to  explore this career change but you also  have time throughout the year I have  classmates who have started companies  I've cost me to have done three 90s with  what we call three 90s similar to this  experience with res ed and because we  have access to Silicon Valley a lot of  folks are you know pitching VCS and and  working working their day to day so I  think those both the alumni network the  Career Center and this internship  day-to-day have really helped me  solidify how to make that change and  whether I want to make that change  okay you touched on something that we  also have a question about which is GSB  location and Mike I don't know if this  has come into your thought process for  recruiting or kind of the more formal  channels at the GSB at you  gotten a sense of how our location and  Silicon Valley is either expanded  opportunities or the challenges that  might come with being on the west coast  sure well I think um so I have a  personal bias towards the west coast  I've been here for yeah almost ten years  and so that was actually a huge selling  point for me for those of you who may  not have been here the weather here is  pretty awesome I would say that  certainly if you know you're you're in  Palo Alto but your desired industry and  company and firms are in New York City  there's no question that that becomes  logistically more difficult you know  flying six hours out to New York flying  back is is more challenging than for  students at some of our peer schools on  the East Coast that doesn't mean however  that those firms aren't interested in  GSB graduates in fact you know a lot of  the demand a lot of the job  opportunities available through the CMC  are from New York employers and so the  the downside to the geography is not  limited access it's actually just  logistical difficulty mm-hmm and that's  something I found when I was  interviewing in New York I was  interviewing with some investment firms  and just flying out there was the most  painful part but it wasn't hard to get  them to be interested in GSB grads in  terms of obviously the benefit is if  you're interested in industries that are  more centered around the bay area you  know biotech information technology  obviously then there is I think the  access and the logistical ease is pretty  unparalleled so it really depends on  what you want but I guess what I would  emphasize is that you know access is  incredibly broad and that that the one  commitment you'd have to make is to  undergo some more logistical challenges  and flying back and forth yeah building  off that I think I I did recruit on the  on the East Coast full-time and I think  two things that actually helped in being  out here surprisingly one is that we  have really long breaks so I would use  those breaks  you network with folks in New York  and really set up in informational  interviews and interviews during those  those periods so you do have yes  logistical nightmares during the school  year but if you're really strategic and  smart about how you use your time off  Stamford gives you a ton of time off to  be able to fly to other places and then  the second thing is I think you're  actually it recruiters are I think a  little bit more surprised to get emails  from folks on the west coast about East  Coast jobs and you know jobs in Chicago  and jobs in Texas and things like that  so I think you actually have or I found  that I actually had somewhat of an  advantage because I was they were like  how would you why would you want to  leave California and so I I found myself  actually I'm recruiting with a smaller  pool of people and so I think yes you do  have the access if you want to stay in  California but if you don't I think both  the the availability of breaks to to  network and then also the fact that most  people do choose to stay in California  you you have your you're a bit of an  anomaly you actually have some increased  opportunity as a result and I think also  the size of our program relatively  compared to the number of opportunities  on the East Coast it's just a  supply-demand perspective there's so few  people I think relatively in the larger  scheme that it creates a supply-demand  advantage for our student body and you  brought up a really good example even  with your significant other working in  real estate in New York having access to  our alums so I also think about networks  more broadly than geography I think a  lot about networks in 2016 kind of being  informational networks and access to  people who really have deep expertise  and I think so much of that I mean of  course you want contextual knowledge but  also learning about the access to  information that you have through our  alums can really cross borders and time  zones pretty easily these days and for  sure so well logistics can be a  challenge we also have a question about  challenges in general at Stanford and  someone's asking us what has been your  most difficult experience at Stanford  it's a head-scratcher sure so I I'm  actually at Stanford with my partner and  we we applied at the same time we were  both really fortunate to be admitted and  and she's also an MBA too and the  biggest challenge for us just on a  personal level was just in the beginning  the the GSB is so all-consuming in terms  of its demands on your time from the  academics to the to the social scene to  you know you're thinking about jobs and  internships and should I start a  business etc etc and I think the biggest  challenge for us and a source of  friction in our relationship in those  early days coming to the GSB was how  much time you know do we spend on  spending time with each other versus on  these other priorities that we have  because we do want to go through the  transformative experience that the GSB  has to offer  and I think it generalizes to a broader  point for me it manifested itself in my  relationship with my significant other  you know I think for people who don't  have partners here that the  generalization is that you know you have  your personal life you have your life  that you lead before the GSB and now you  have these massive time commitments that  are put on you by the GSB and the  question of how you balance those two  can be fraught with uncertainty and can  cause frictions with with with your  relationships with people before before  you came I think everyone I know has has  successfully worked through those my  partner and I were able to you know find  the right balance but in the beginning  that can be a really big challenge  mm-hmm yeah and just building off of  that I think I think my biggest  challenge was the transition Fall  Quarter as well for slightly different  reasons I think I came in knowing what I  wanted to focus on but the second you  get here you're exposed to so much and  you're exposed to so many different  classmates and you hear about a company  that someone's been at and you're like  wow that sounds interesting maybe I'll  add a third industry to look at and so I  think for me  it was really figuring out how do I  learn to say no to things and be  comfortable getting out of the  experience what I came here to get out  and so I would say the the more clear  you can be about what you want from  Business School and the closer you can  stick to that when you're here the  easier that adjustment is but I would  say yeah that the first quarter at the  GSB there there's a lot going on and  there's a lot of opportunity it's really  critical to focus on what you decided  you wanted to get out of the experience  yeah that's perfect I really appreciate  you adding some advice in there because  I was gonna ask if you had any final  tips so that's really great words to  live by not just for the application but  I think for life mic'd any final  thoughts on tips coming into either  applying to or attending the GSB yeah  absolutely  so my final word of advice for the  guests who are watching over the  Internet is that a lot of the admissions  process and even the GSB process itself  if you haven't been a part of if you  have experienced that yet it can seem  contrived it can seem like what what is  what do they actually mean by you know  being introspective and being reflective  like aren't those aren't those just buzz  words right and and what I would say is  they can be and they can be and that  that only happens if you don't truly  kind of buy in if you don't truly commit  yourself to being reflective right to  thinking about why have you made the  decisions you did being honest about  that being honest about where you're  from who you are what your goals are  instead of you know what you think that  society or admissions officers want to  see from you or if you're admitted what  you think your peers or your professors  want to see from you the only way you  really get to experience the GSB x'  benefits at a true level of depth is is  when you buy in is when you say I am  going to do these things even though it  feels kind of awkward and cheesy at  first I am gonna reflect I am gonna  think about my life as it's been so far  and  what I hope for it to be like in the  future so that would be my my final word  advice and the the buzzword that I would  use to capture that is be authentic be  authentic to yourself those are great  tips both for the application and for  life I wanted to thank you both so much  for taking the time I know you always  have 50 things you could be doing I mean  half hour of the day so I really  appreciate it and you really brought the  GSB to life so thank you so much all of  you that are joining us I want to thank  you for your time as well really  appreciate you taking the time to learn  more about the MBA program as a reminder  you can always learn more about the GSB  on our website and we'll also be on the  road this summer so if you want to learn  more about Stanford in person you can  take a look at our event calendar online
 

